7:**Units:** Always ppb (µg/kg) for food matrices unless a source uses different units, in which case convert and note the original. Always specify wet weight vs dry weight.
9:**Metal names:** Use Pb, Cd, iAs, tAs, MeHg, tHg, Ni, Al, Cr, Cr-VI, Sn, Sb, U. Spell out on first use per page. The iAs vs tAs distinction is non-negotiable; never conflate them. The tHg vs MeHg distinction is also non-negotiable. Total Cr must never be treated as Cr-VI unless the source speciates hexavalent chromium.
17:Format: `<firstauthor><year>-<slug>`, lowercase, hyphenated. One cite key per source, globally unique. Disambiguate collisions with `-a`, `-b`, etc.
23:**Frontmatter wikilinks:** Page-reference arrays in frontmatter use the quoted-wikilink form `["[[folder/slug]]", "[[folder/slug]]"]`. Quoting is required because YAML reserves `[` as a flow-sequence delimiter; without quotes, `[[` is parsed as a nested empty list. Wrapping page-reference arrays as wikilinks is what makes Obsidian's graph view surface relationships sourced from frontmatter.
26:- `source_refs` → `[[sources/...]]`
39:**Numbers:** Always include sample size and method when citing a concentration. "Rice has high arsenic" is not a wiki-quality claim; "FDA TDS 2014 found mean iAs of 92 ppb (n=76) in white rice by ICP-MS with HPLC speciation" is. Verify every numeric claim against the source before writing it. Do not round, approximate, or harmonize values across sources; preserve the actual numbers.
41:**Regulatory values:** Never silently substitute or round a regulatory threshold. FDA's action level for iAs in infant rice cereal is 100 ppb, not "approximately 100 ppb" and not "about 0.1 ppm." Cite the exact value from the agency source and note the unit the agency used. If converting units for comparison, show both.
43:**Uncertainty:** When you do not know or sources disagree, say so. Under-claim rather than over-claim. This is a certification and consumer-safety domain; false confidence is the biggest risk.
49:Every source gets an `evidence_tier`.
55:Synthesis pages should lean on A-tier, use B-tier with attribution, and treat C-tier as leads to verify rather than as evidence. Courses and certification criteria should cite A-tier sources.
57:Flag contradictions explicitly. If a B-tier source conflicts with an A-tier source, note both and explain. Do not silently average.
61:Organized by category. Each entry: `- [[path/page]] — one-line summary (N sources).`
